<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Cid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1043–1099</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Cid, Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar, was a Castilian nobleman and soldier. Exiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>, he served the Muslim rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and collected tribute from other taifas in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. In 1094 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and ruled it as an independent lordship until his death. He became the hero of the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantar-de-mio-cid">Cantar de mio Cid</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-cid.jpg" alt="El Cid" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: photographer: ElCaminodeSantiago09 2006 · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatua_del_Cid_(Burgos).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
